            <div xml:id="d880">

                <pb facs="0935" n="837" sameAs="frus:frus1920v01/pg837"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">818.00/990: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at San José</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Chase</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >San José</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-07-28T16:00:00-05:36"><hi rendition="#italic">July 28, 1920—4
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received July 29—7:14
                    a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>44. My July 21, 4 p.m. Please examine enclosures with my despatch number 790 June
                        4th.<anchor n="7" xml:id="d880-f1"/> The President has called a meeting of
                    citizens for next Saturday to advise as to whether the law should be approved.
                    This is reputed to be because of fear on account of the very strong
                    representations made by the British Government as <pb facs="0936" n="838"
                        xml:id="pg838"/> to the Amory concession and the Royal Bank of Canada claim,
                    the latter reported as practically an ultimatum.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Chase</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
